\subsection{Main Results and Component Decomposition}
\label{sec:main_results}

Table~\ref{tab:main} compares Plan~A (Section~\ref{sec:adaptive_k_method}) with analysis baselines that isolate the contribution of selection and correction at fixed $K$.

\paragraph{Plan~A: selective correction at $K{=}2$.}
\looseness=-1 The proposed pipeline achieves $18.3 \pm 2.1$\% on Object (3 seeds) and $35.0 \pm 1.0$\% on Spatial, exceeding the best fixed-$K$ baseline (CRM $K{=}10$: $16.8$\%, $34.4$\%) with 80\% fewer VLA forward passes. The CRM trigger rate is task-dependent: 83.7\% on Object (16.3\% fast-path steps) and 97.6\% on Spatial, where correction is almost universally beneficial. Selective application outperforms unconditional correction at $K{=}10$, consistent with over-triggering degrading performance (P25 $<$ P50, Table~\ref{tab:adaptive}). With $K{=}2$, the centroid has lower variance than at $K{=}10$, providing a cleaner starting point for bounded correction.

